{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794585754074,"sessionID":"ses_e2a5adb61ffe5JLlq3JICE94gJ","part":{"id":"prt_1d5a579da001Xe1BZ4eR7oPNlM","sessionID":"ses_e2a5adb61ffe5JLlq3JICE94gJ","messageID":"msg_1d5a573ba001S4AMHXGR68Ym9L","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794585755577,"sessionID":"ses_e2a5adb61ffe5JLlq3JICE94gJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1d5a57b39001TddL4vtKt6UCjw","sessionID":"ses_e2a5adb61ffe5JLlq3JICE94gJ","messageID":"msg_1d5a573ba001S4AMHXGR68Ym9L","type":"tool","id":"call_57718042bbdb46f28f9e3942","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/SKILL.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/SKILL.md, lines 1-389\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-changelog\n3: description: Author global component changelogs in the v4 narrative style, with placement, versioning, lean content and release notes.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.5.0.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # create-changelog\n9: \n10: `create-changelog` is the changelog-authoring workflow packet of the `sk-doc` family. It writes a global component changelog under `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`. Packet-local (nested) changelogs under a spec packet's `changelog/` folder are deprecated, and this workflow no longer writes them (section 4).\n11: \n12: This file is the executable contract: resolve the source, resolve the global target, calculate the version, generate, validate and write. `assets/changelog-template.md` owns what an entry says and how it reads. The `references/` set, routed by `references/README.md`, holds worked examples and edge cases.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n17: \n18: ### Activation Triggers\n19: \n20: Use this workflow to:\n21: \n22: 1. Create a global component changelog at `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`.\n23: 2. Answer a request for a packet-local (nested) changelog: refuse it as deprecated and offer a global component entry instead.\n24: 3. Run or support `/create:changelog`.\n25: 4. Resolve a changed spec folder, component hint or recent git history into a changelog target.\n26: 5. Calculate the next four-part version for a global changelog.\n27: 6. Prepare GitHub release notes from a generated global changelog.\n28: \n29: Keyword triggers: `create changelog`, `/create:changelog`, `changelog`, `changelog entry`, `release notes`, `global changelog`, `packet-local changelog`, `nested changelog`, `since the last version`, `vX.Y.Z.B`.\n30: \n31: ### When NOT to Use\n32: \n33: 1. A release plan with no file output.\n34: 2. A README (`create-readme`), agent, command, benchmark, feature catalog, manual testing playbook or skill. Each has its own `create-*` packet. A flowchart or any other diagram belongs to the `sk-design` hub (`/design:diagram`), which this hub does not register a packet for.\n35: 3. Auditing, scoring or optimizing an existing changelog (`create-quality-control`).\n36: 4. A source too ambiguous to resolve to a spec folder, a component hint or recent git history.\n37: 5. Branch, commit or PR work and release mechanics beyond `--release`, which `sk-git` owns. The tag and GitHub release run in the command YAMLs' release step (section 8), and only for the `skilled` release line.\n38: \n39: ---\n40: \n41: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n42: \n43: The `sk-doc` hub resolves a changelog-authoring request through `mode-registry.json`.\n44: `hub-router.json` scores the request, the winning `workflowMode` is `sk-create-changelog`, and the hub loads this `SKILL.md` first.\n45: A request to create a global component changelog, write release notes, calculate the next four-part version or resolve a changed source selects this packet.\n46: A packet-local changelog request that lands here is refused as deprecated and offered a global entry.\n47: The packet has one output mode, the global component entry.\n48: \n49: `references/README.md` is the route map and the fallback load, and `assets/changelog-template.md` owns what an entry says and how it reads.\n50: \n51: A request that matches no registered mode returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`, never a silent default.\n52: \n53: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n54: \n55: ---\n56: \n57: ## 3. REQUIRED INPUTS\n58: \n59: | Input | Required | Meaning |\n60: | --- | --- | --- |\n61: | `source_type` | Yes, resolved before writing | `spec_folder`, `component` or `git_history` |\n62: | `spec_folder` | When `source_type = spec_folder` | The spec folder to summarize into a global component entry |\n63: | `component_hint` | When `source_type = component` | A name matched against the discovered changelog folders |\n64: | `version_bump` | Optional, default `auto` | `major`, `minor`, `patch`, `build` or `auto` |\n65: | `--nested` | Refused | Packet-local changelogs are deprecated. The workflow stops and offers a global entry (section 4) |\n66: | `--release` | Optional | After a `skilled` release entry is written, tags the version and publishes the GitHub release with the entry as its body (section 8). Any other component skips the release |\n67: \n68: Global output lives under `.skilled/changelog/`. Versions read `v{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH}.{BUILD}`, the first one is `v1.0.0.0` and dates use `YYYY-MM-DD`. Between 40 and 79 percent confidence in the source or target, proceed and record the assumptions. Below 40 percent, stop and ask with A/B/C options.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 4. HOW IT WORKS: CHANGELOG OUTPUT MODES\n73: \n74: ### Global Component Changelog\n75: \n76: A global entry is written to `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`.\n77: `{component}` is a lowercase kebab-case segment matching `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`, or `{hub}/{link}` when the folder is a hub, with each segment matching that pattern.\n78: Reject an ambiguous hint rather than emit a guessed or underscore-bearing directory.\n79: The `v{VERSION}.md` filename is an exact version contract and is never slug-normalized.\n80: \n81: | Bump | Calculation | Source trigger intent |\n82: | --- | --- | --- |\n83: | `major` | `{MAJOR+1}.0.0.0` | Breaking, overhaul, rewrite, migration, platform version jump |\n84: | `minor` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR+1}.0.0` | Significant new feature or subsystem addition |\n85: | `patch` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH+1}.0` | Incremental improvement, bug fix, refactor, docs, cleanup |\n86: | `build` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH}.{BUILD+1}` | Hotfix, typo, same-day build on an already-published version |\n87: \n88: Auto detection takes the explicit `--bump` flag first, then change-type keywords in the `spec.md` title and purpose, then conventional commit prefixes. With no clear signal it defaults to `patch`. If the calculated file already exists, increment the build segment until the path is unique.\n89: \n90: ### Packet-Local Changelogs (Deprecated)\n91: \n92: Spec folders no longer get changelogs. Changelogs live only under `.skilled/changelog/` and in each skill's own `changelog/` folder. This workflow writes no file under `specs/**/changelog/` and refuses `--nested`.\n93: \n94: Existing packet-local files such as `changelog-<packet>-root.md` stay where they are and still validate. The spec-kit nested generator remains only to render them and prints a deprecation notice when it runs.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. CHANGELOG FORMAT CONTRACT\n99: \n100: Read `assets/changelog-template.md` before generating global content. It owns the compact and expanded shapes (section 2), what to leave out and the say-it-once rule (section 3), voice and length (section 4) and format selection (section 5). Every entry must read in the voice and section shape of the three model entries, at whatever length the template's ceilings allow. `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md` models sentence length, plain words, feature detail and the testing section. `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.1.0.md` models selection and a release with many changes, and `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.0.0.md` models voice and how a major release opens.\n101: \n102: The contract in one pass:\n103: \n104: 1. Frontmatter first, then an optional editorial title H1, then the summary narrative. No version header, backlink or version-date line.\n105: 2. Compact under 10 reader-visible changes when the release is neither major nor breaking: summary, `## What's New at a Glance` and `## Upgrade`, with `## Why This Release` only when the summary does not say why.\n106: 3. Expanded for 10 or more changes, a major bump or any breaking change: opening narrative, `## Why This Release`, `## What's New at a Glance`, topical H2 sections with benefit-led H4 items, `## How It Was Checked` and `## Upgrade Notes`.\n107: 4. A spec-folder blockquote, ``> Spec folder: `{path}` ``, when the release has a spec folder. It carries the path only, and the level stays in the packet.\n108: 5. Content chosen by reader impact and each fact said once. No file or schema tables unless the numbers are the story.\n109: 6. Detail by default, with no request needed. Each new feature says how it works and shows a real line of output. Every fix a reader could run into is named. `## How It Was Checked` gives the before and after, the cost and the tests that ran.\n110: 7. Short sentences in plain words: about 8 words each, none past 22 and 10 at most on average.\n111: \n112: When a source such as an older command snippet conflicts with the template, follow the template, write the narrative format and record the mismatch rather than inventing a hybrid.\n113: \n114: ### Frontmatter Contract\n115: \n116: A global entry must be as findable as a spec document, so its frontmatter block carries these five keys, in this order:\n117: \n118: | Key | Value |\n119: | --- | --- |\n120: | `title` | `<component> v<version>, <editorial title>`, or `<component> v<version>` when the entry has no editorial title. A Skilled release entry uses its H1, or `Skilled v<version>` |\n121: | `description` | One or two plain sentences, 250 characters at most, saying what the release changed |\n122: | `trigger_phrases` | The identity phrases first, then one or two topic phrases |\n123: | `importance_tier` | `\"normal\"` |\n124: | `contextType` | `\"general\"` |\n125: \n126: Identity phrases name the entry the way a reader asks for it.\n127: A skill entry declares `<component> v<version>` and `<component> <version>`, where `<component>` is the `name:` in the `SKILL.md` of the folder that holds the entry's changelog folder, never a `{hub}/{link}` target.\n128: The deep-loop runtime has no `SKILL.md` and is `deep-loop-runtime`.\n129: A Skilled release entry declares `v<version> release notes` and `skilled v<version>`.\n130: A topic phrase is 2 to 6 words from the entry's own text that name what changed.\n131: It carries no version number and never fits any release the way \"bug fixes\" would.\n132: A component entry may keep a `version:` key after the five.\n133: \n134: Existing packet-local entries keep the block the deprecated nested generator renders, with an identity phrase derived from the output path.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. TOPOLOGY AND TARGET RESOLUTION\n139: \n140: ### Mode Detection\n141: \n142: For a spec folder, read `implementation-summary.md` first and `spec.md` for the title, purpose and level. Read `tasks.md` only to confirm what shipped. Every source resolves to global mode:\n143: \n144: 1. `--nested` present: stop, say packet-local changelogs are deprecated and offer a global entry.\n145: 2. A spec folder, including a phase child, a phase parent or a folder that already has `changelog/`, resolves to the global component its changed paths touch (Global Component Discovery below).\n146: 3. A component hint or git history source is built from recent commits and the files they touched.\n147: \n148: ### Global Component Discovery\n149: \n150: Discover component folders from the repository at run time with `ls -d .skilled/changelog/*/ 2>/dev/null | sort`, never from a hardcoded table. Then resolve:\n151: \n152: 1. Each folder is a plain component name, such as `sk-doc` or `system-spec-kit`, with no numeric prefix. One folder is not a skill: `skilled` is the framework release line, with one entry per Skilled release.\n153: 2. Match changed paths against folder names by whole path segment, never by substring.\n154: 3. Match `.skilled/skills/{name}/**` to the folder named `{name}`, and `.skilled/commands/**` or `.skilled/agents/**` to the folder that owns them.\n155: 4. Resolve a hub to one of its links, never to the hub folder.\n156:    A hub is a real folder other than `skilled`, such as `sk-doc` or `sk-code`, holding one link per member packet plus `parent`, the hub's own changelog.\n157:    `ls -l .skilled/changelog/{hub}/` lists them.\n158:    A path under `.skilled/skills/{hub}/{packet}/` resolves to the link whose target is `{packet}/changelog`.\n159:    Any other path under the hub, and a hint that names the hub, resolve to `{hub}/parent`.\n160:    A hint that names a member by its link, its packet folder or `{hub}/{link}` resolves to that link.\n161: 5. Match `component_hint` against folder names exactly.\n162: 6. Use spec path segments as a tiebreaker.\n163: 7. Resolve to `skilled` only when `component_hint` names it. A changed path never selects it, because the operator chooses a framework release.\n164: 8. If nothing matches, do not invent or default a folder. Pause and ask which component the changelog belongs to.\n165: \n166: Then choose the target:\n167: \n168: 1. A component with more than 60 percent of the changed files is primary.\n169: 2. With several roughly equal components, pick the primary by file count and list the rest as secondary.\n170: 3. Verify the target folder exists before writing, and never create a missing one.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 7. CREATION WORKFLOW\n175: \n176: Complete these seven steps in order. The command YAMLs number the same workflow as eight steps, because they run the optional GitHub release as its own step 7.\n177: \n178: Before writing, validate each authored component, packet or phase slug. Exact version filenames and frozen changelog paths stay exempt.\n179: \n180: ```bash\n181: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <component-or-phase-slug>\n182: ```\n183: \n184: 1. **Analyze context.** Resolve the source type and read the source (section 6). Compile `work_context`: what changed for the reader, the change type, the level and the source.\n185: 2. **Resolve output target.** Discover the folders, match paths and hints (section 6), choose the primary component, list secondary ones and verify `.skilled/changelog/{resolved_folder}/` exists.\n186: 3. **Determine version.** Do these in order:\n187:    - List the `v*.md` entries in the target folder and in its generation folders (`v1+/`, `v2+/` and so on).\n188:    - List the version entries on the upstream branch with `git ls-tree`, which reads the branch's upstream, else `origin/main`.\n189:    For the `skilled` release line with at least one entry, first check the highest entry for a release tag with `git ls-remote --exit-code --tags origin refs/tags/v{latest}`:\n190:    - Exit 2 means no tag, so that release is still open. Extend that entry wherever it sits instead of numbering a new one, keep its version and skip the bump.\n191:    - A bump the operator chose is not applied, and the report says so, and says that tagging the open release first starts a new one.\n192:    - When the open entry exists only upstream, stop and ask for a merge or rebase first.\n193:    - Any exit other than 0 or 2 means the check failed, so number a new entry and say so rather than risk editing a release that already shipped.\n194:    Every other component numbers a new entry.\n195:    - Start from the highest of the local and upstream `vX.Y.Z.B` versions, choose the bump (section 4) and calculate the next version.\n196:    - Increment the build segment while the file exists locally or upstream, because two branches numbering from their own checkout pick the same version and collide at merge.\n197:    - Without a reachable upstream, number locally and say so in the report.\n198: 4. **Generate content.** Read `assets/changelog-template.md`, then:\n199:    - Write the frontmatter block per the Frontmatter Contract in section 5.\n200:    - Choose the content before writing it (template section 3). Start from what the reader would notice, act on or decide differently about, confirm each against the source, merge items with the same effect and drop the housekeeping.\n201:    - Select compact or expanded from the changes that survive (template section 5).\n202:    - Write each section to its one job, so a fact appears once: themes in the opening, the gap in Why This Release, one payoff line per theme at a glance, the detail in the H4 items and only actions in the upgrade notes.\n203:    - Explain how each new feature works, step by step, and quote one real line of its output. Name every fix a reader could run into.\n204:    - Write `## How It Was Checked` from evidence the source records: the before and after of a real command, the measured cost and each suite's result. Run the command when the source lacks its output. Never write a number that no run produced, and say so when something was not measured.\n205:    - Write short sentences in everyday words (template section 4). Split a long sentence rather than cut its fact.\n206:    - Lead with why the release matters. Outside How It Was Checked, add a table only when its numbers are the story.\n207:    - Order the opening, the glance list, the topical sections and the upgrade notes by what matters most to the reader, never by when the work landed.\n208:    - When extending an open release, read the entry first and fold the new work into it.\n209:      Add its section or H4 items, a glance bullet in section order, its spec folder on the spec-folder line and any upgrade action.\n210:      Widen the opening and Why This Release only enough to cover the new theme, keep the title unless the new work changes the headline, and correct any existing sentence the new work makes untrue.\n211:      When the new work matters more to a reader than what the entry leads with, retitle the entry and re-rank its sections, so the release still opens on its most important change.\n212: 5. **Validate quality.** Run the section 9 checks on the draft. For a new entry, confirm the version is strictly greater than the latest one and no file exists at the target. For an extension, confirm the target is the open entry. Confirm every path the entry names exists. Apply the cut test (template section 3), fix what is safe and revalidate.\n213: 6. **Write the file.** Write `.skilled/changelog/{primary_component}/v{next_version}.md`, or edit the open entry in place when extending, then read back its first lines.\n214:    Set `next_version` in every version carrier the component's skill root already has (`SKILL.md`, `description.json`, `hub-router.json` and `mode-registry.json`), never in `README.md` or a hub `ROUTER.md`, whose versions are each document's own.\n215:    For a hub, run `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs` on the hub and stop on a non-zero exit.\n216:    Report secondary components as candidates rather than writing extra files.\n217: 7. **Report and preserve context.** Report the path, component, version, whether the entry is new or extended, bump type, summary, section count and H4 item count. For a `skilled` entry, also report the release: its URL when one is published, or that none is and the `--release` run that publishes it. When the entry belongs to a published release, bring the release in step first (section 8). A spec-folder source can preserve context through the normal memory save workflow. Do not claim completion before the written file is verified.\n218: \n219: Pause when confidence drops below 40 percent, component resolution stays ambiguous, the calculated version looks wrong or a required section would be empty.\n220: \n221: ---\n222: \n223: ## 8. NOTATION AND FORMAT RULES\n224: \n225: The voice, structure, omission and length rules live in `assets/changelog-template.md` sections 3 and 4. The non-negotiables are:\n226: \n227: 1. Write for a smart person who is not a developer, and lead with why the release matters.\n228: 2. Keep what the reader would notice or act on, how each feature works, every fix and the evidence in How It Was Checked. Drop file inventories, test counts outside that section, internal labels, process detail and follow-on housekeeping, and compress reverted work to one story sentence or nothing.\n229: 3. Say each fact once, in the section whose job it is.\n230: 4. Bold lead-in sentences on glance bullets, a trailing `<!-- section: {Topical Domain} -->` comment on each one in an expanded entry, H4 item headings of 2-7 words (10 at most), an `&nbsp;` line before every H2 (the opening, Why This Release, What's New at a Glance and every later section), nothing between H4 items, no `---` rule in the body and inline `**Breaking:**` markers.\n231: 5. No Oxford commas, em dashes or semicolons, per the Human Voice Rules.\n232: 6. Short sentences in everyday words. No sentence runs past 22 words and the entry averages 10 at most.\n233: \n234: ### Release Notes\n235: \n236: For GitHub release notes, use the changelog content with any YAML frontmatter and the editorial title H1 removed, keeping its spacing, then append an `&nbsp;` line and the pointer. `scripts/build_release_notes.py <entry> --out <file>` builds exactly this body, so a publish and every later amend match the entry:\n237: \n238: ```text\n239: &nbsp;\n240: \n241: Full changelog: `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`\n242: ```\n243: \n244: The command YAMLs own the release itself, as `step_7_publish_release`.\n245: They run it only when `publish_release` is explicitly true and the component is `skilled`, the framework release line.\n246: Any other component skips the release, because component versions share the `vX.X.X.X` shape with release tags and a component version must never become one.\n247: The tag is `v{next_version}`, and the release title is the tag followed by the entry's editorial title, the H1 without its leading version.\n248: The step checks for a tag collision and an authenticated `gh`, then runs `git tag -a`, `git push origin {release_tag}` and `gh release create {release_tag} --title {release_title} --notes-file {notes_file}`, which publishes at once with no draft stage.\n249: `:confirm` shows the exact commands and runs them only after approval.\n250: \n251: #### Keep a Published Release in Step\n252: \n253: A published release must always show its entry as it reads now. Whenever an entry changes after its release is published, bring the release in step in the same run, without waiting to be asked:\n254: \n255: 1. Rebuild the body with `build_release_notes.py`.\n256: 2. Run `gh release edit {release_tag} --notes-file {notes_file}`, adding `--title {release_title}` when the editorial title changed.\n257: 3. Check the tag: `git show {release_tag}:{entry_path}` must match the entry on the default branch.\n258: 4. Report the release URL from `gh release view {release_tag} --json url`.\n259: \n260: When step 3 shows a difference, the tag points at a commit that holds older text or older code. Recommend a republish, so the tag, the entry and the release agree.\n261: \n262: Republishing deletes and recreates a public ref, so it needs the operator's yes each time. Never run it as a default or in an unattended run. Ask once, in the same reply as the report, with republish as the recommended answer. Name the old tag object and say it can be pushed back. Only a request that names the tag move counts as the yes, such as \"republish the tag\" or \"move the tag\". A request to publish or edit the notes does not. The body edit in step 2 needs no such wait.\n263: \n264: After the yes, run these commands in order. The local tag goes too, or `git tag -a` fails because it still exists:\n265: \n266: ```bash\n267: gh release delete {release_tag} --cleanup-tag --yes\n268: git tag -d {release_tag}\n269: git tag -a {release_tag} -m \"{tag_message}\"\n270: git push origin {release_tag}\n271: gh release create {release_tag} --title \"{release_title}\" --notes-file {notes_file} --latest --verify-tag\n272: ```\n273: \n274: ---\n275: \n276: ## 9. VALIDATION\n277: \n278: Validate the target, version and content before writing.\n279: \n280: Global changelog checks:\n281: \n282: 1. The target is `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md` in an existing folder, and no file exists at that path. Extending an open `skilled` release is the one exception: the target is that release's existing entry.\n283: 2. The version follows `vX.Y.Z.B` and is strictly greater than the latest one in the folder, counting its generation folders. An extension keeps the open release's version.\n284: 3. The frontmatter carries `title`, `description`, `trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier` and `contextType` in contract order, with both identity phrases for the target path and at least one topic phrase. `validate_document.py` blocks an entry whose block, keys or phrases are missing.\n285: 4. The prose opens with the summary narrative, and the spec-folder blockquote is present when the source is a spec folder.\n286: 5. The tier's sections are present: compact carries the summary, `## What's New at a Glance` and `## Upgrade`. Expanded carries the opening narrative, `## Why This Release`, `## What's New at a Glance`, at least one topical H2 with H4 items, `## How It Was Checked` and `## Upgrade Notes`.\n287: 6. No Files Changed, Test Impact or Schema Changes section. Any table outside How It Was Checked passes the earned-evidence rule.\n288: 7. Omission applied: no file inventories, process detail or housekeeping items, and no test counts outside How It Was Checked. No internal labels (task, requirement, scenario or finding IDs, packet or phase numbers) appear outside the spec-folder line.\n289: 8. Each fact appears once: no sentence restates one from another section, and each glance bullet adds something the summary did not.\n290: 9. The template's ceilings hold: opening within 5 paragraphs, prose near its word budgets (guidance, counted in words, never sentences), at most 14 glance bullets, H4 headings within 10 words, compact files within 40 prose lines, expanded files within 280 lines below the frontmatter and expanded entries within 32 H4 items.\n291: 10. H4 headings are benefit-led and never numbered or sentence-length, and no H3 sits inside a topical section.\n292: 11. `scripts/check_changelog_shape.py` passes the draft. It fails an expanded entry past 280 lines or 32 H4 items, more than 14 glance bullets, tagged glance bullets out of section order and a spec-folder line with a level label. It also fails a sentence past 22 words, an average past 10 words a sentence and an expanded entry with no `## How It Was Checked`.\n293: \n294: Voice gate, run on the draft before writing:\n295: \n296: ```bash\n297: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/scripts/hvr_scan.py <draft-file>\n298: ```\n299: \n300: The scan must report zero hard blockers (banned punctuation or words).\n301: Deductions below that threshold are acceptable.\n302: If the scanner is unavailable, apply the rules by hand and record that the scan was skipped.\n303: Structural checks complement it:\n304: - bold lead-ins on glance bullets.\n305: - an `&nbsp;` line before every H2.\n306: - nothing between H4 items.\n307: - no `---` rule in the body.\n308: - no `**Problem:**`/`**Fix:**` labels.\n309: - inline `**Breaking:**` markers.\n310: \n311: Shape gate, run on the draft before writing:\n312: \n313: ```bash\n314: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/scripts/check_changelog_shape.py <draft-file>\n315: ```\n316: \n317: Exit 1 prints one line per broken rule and blocks the write. Cut at the H4 level (template section 3) and run it again. The order rule is skipped, with a note, when no glance bullet carries a section tag.\n318: \n319: After writing, run the shared markdown checks:\n320: \n321: ```bash\n322: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <written-file>\n323: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <written-file>\n324: ```\n325: \n326: If validation fails, fix the blocking issues or report the exact blocker and command output.\n327: \n328: Packet-local files are deprecated, so no new one is validated here. `validate_document.py` still checks the existing ones under `specs/**/changelog/`.\n329: \n330: ---\n331: \n332: ## 10. RULES\n333: \n334: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n335: \n336: 1. Follow the seven-step workflow in order.\n337: 2. Read `assets/changelog-template.md` before generating global content.\n338: 3. Discover global component folders from `.skilled/changelog/` at run time, and resolve to an existing one before writing.\n339: 4. Keep global versions four-part and strictly sequential. Extending an untagged `skilled` release keeps its version.\n340: 5. Choose content by reader impact and say each fact once.\n341: 6. Refuse `--nested` and route a spec-folder source to a global component entry.\n342: 7. List secondary affected components.\n343: 8. Verify the written file before claiming completion.\n344: 9. Keep this packet self-contained, with the advisor identity at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n345: 10. Write the feature detail, the fixes and How It Was Checked without being asked for them.\n346: 11. Bring a published release in step with its entry in the same run, and end the report with the release URL.\n347: \n348: ### ⛔ NEVER\n349: \n350: 1. Add a packet-local `graph-metadata.json`.\n351: 2. Overwrite an existing changelog or reuse an existing version. Extending an untagged `skilled` release entry is the one exception, and it adds to the entry rather than replacing it.\n352: 3. Guess a component folder, or create a missing one.\n353: 4. Write an entry with an empty summary or an empty required section.\n354: 5. Write a new packet-local changelog, or use the deprecated nested generator to create one. Its `--write` only re-renders an existing packet changelog.\n355: 6. Make the command router or YAML the only workflow contract.\n356: 7. Hide a source-format conflict. Record it and follow the template.\n357: \n358: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n359: \n360: 1. No source context can be found in a spec folder, component history or git history.\n361: 2. Component resolution stays ambiguous after file-count and path-segment analysis.\n362: 3. The calculated version is unexpected or cannot be made unique.\n363: 4. A required section would be empty.\n364: 5. The user asks for a GitHub release with no git release workflow or command context available.\n365: 6. Validation still fails after safe local fixes.\n366: \n367: ---\n368: \n369: ## 11. REFERENCES\n370: \n371: 1. `assets/changelog-template.md`: the canonical format, selection, voice and length rules.\n372: 2. `scripts/check_changelog_shape.py`: the shape gate that enforces the expanded ceilings, the glance order and the spec-folder line.\n373:    `scripts/build_release_notes.py`: builds the GitHub release body from an entry, for a publish and for keeping a published release in step.\n374: 3. `references/README.md`: the route map to `worked-examples.md` (filled-in entries), `version-bump-rules.md` (concrete version choices) and `topology-edge-cases.md` (placement, back-dating, source conflicts and the release flow).\n375: 4. `.skilled/commands/create/changelog.md`: the thin `/create:changelog` router.\n376: 5. `.skilled/commands/create/assets/create-changelog-auto.yaml` and `create-changelog-confirm.yaml`: the `:auto` and `:confirm` workflows, which resolve to this same contract.\n377: \n378: ---\n379: \n380: ## 12. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n381: \n382: The workflow succeeds when:\n383: \n384: 1. The file lands at its global component path, and a `--nested` request is refused with a global entry offered instead.\n385: 2. Global versioning is sequential, unique and four-part, and an untagged `skilled` release is extended rather than followed by a second untagged entry.\n386: 3. The entry follows the template's compact or expanded shape and reads in the v4 style: why first, benefit-led headings, short plain sentences, how each feature works, every fix, the evidence in How It Was Checked and each fact said once.\n387: 4. A release body uses the entry and appends the full changelog path, and a published release never shows older text than its entry.\n388: 5. Validation passes, or the remaining issue is escalated with exact evidence.\n389: 6. No other file changes unless the user asked for it.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/SKILL.md, lines 1-389\n1: ---\n2: name: sk-create-changelog\n3: description: Author global component changelogs in the v4 narrative style, with placement, versioning, lean content and release notes.\n4: allowed-tools: [Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob]\n5: version: 1.5.0.0\n6: ---\n7: \n8: # create-changelog\n9: \n10: `create-changelog` is the changelog-authoring workflow packet of the `sk-doc` family. It writes a global component changelog under `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`. Packet-local (nested) changelogs under a spec packet's `changelog/` folder are deprecated, and this workflow no longer writes them (section 4).\n11: \n12: This file is the executable contract: resolve the source, resolve the global target, calculate the version, generate, validate and write. `assets/changelog-template.md` owns what an entry says and how it reads. The `references/` set, routed by `references/README.md`, holds worked examples and edge cases.\n13: \n14: ---\n15: \n16: ## 1. WHEN TO USE\n17: \n18: ### Activation Triggers\n19: \n20: Use this workflow to:\n21: \n22: 1. Create a global component changelog at `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`.\n23: 2. Answer a request for a packet-local (nested) changelog: refuse it as deprecated and offer a global component entry instead.\n24: 3. Run or support `/create:changelog`.\n25: 4. Resolve a changed spec folder, component hint or recent git history into a changelog target.\n26: 5. Calculate the next four-part version for a global changelog.\n27: 6. Prepare GitHub release notes from a generated global changelog.\n28: \n29: Keyword triggers: `create changelog`, `/create:changelog`, `changelog`, `changelog entry`, `release notes`, `global changelog`, `packet-local changelog`, `nested changelog`, `since the last version`, `vX.Y.Z.B`.\n30: \n31: ### When NOT to Use\n32: \n33: 1. A release plan with no file output.\n34: 2. A README (`create-readme`), agent, command, benchmark, feature catalog, manual testing playbook or skill. Each has its own `create-*` packet. A flowchart or any other diagram belongs to the `sk-design` hub (`/design:diagram`), which this hub does not register a packet for.\n35: 3. Auditing, scoring or optimizing an existing changelog (`create-quality-control`).\n36: 4. A source too ambiguous to resolve to a spec folder, a component hint or recent git history.\n37: 5. Branch, commit or PR work and release mechanics beyond `--release`, which `sk-git` owns. The tag and GitHub release run in the command YAMLs' release step (section 8), and only for the `skilled` release line.\n38: \n39: ---\n40: \n41: ## 2. SMART ROUTING\n42: \n43: The `sk-doc` hub resolves a changelog-authoring request through `mode-registry.json`.\n44: `hub-router.json` scores the request, the winning `workflowMode` is `sk-create-changelog`, and the hub loads this `SKILL.md` first.\n45: A request to create a global component changelog, write release notes, calculate the next four-part version or resolve a changed source selects this packet.\n46: A packet-local changelog request that lands here is refused as deprecated and offered a global entry.\n47: The packet has one output mode, the global component entry.\n48: \n49: `references/README.md` is the route map and the fallback load, and `assets/changelog-template.md` owns what an entry says and how it reads.\n50: \n51: A request that matches no registered mode returns `UNKNOWN_FALLBACK`, never a silent default.\n52: \n53: Read [`ROUTER.md`](./ROUTER.md) before selecting skill-local resources; open only the matched files.\n54: \n55: ---\n56: \n57: ## 3. REQUIRED INPUTS\n58: \n59: | Input | Required | Meaning |\n60: | --- | --- | --- |\n61: | `source_type` | Yes, resolved before writing | `spec_folder`, `component` or `git_history` |\n62: | `spec_folder` | When `source_type = spec_folder` | The spec folder to summarize into a global component entry |\n63: | `component_hint` | When `source_type = component` | A name matched against the discovered changelog folders |\n64: | `version_bump` | Optional, default `auto` | `major`, `minor`, `patch`, `build` or `auto` |\n65: | `--nested` | Refused | Packet-local changelogs are deprecated. The workflow stops and offers a global entry (section 4) |\n66: | `--release` | Optional | After a `skilled` release entry is written, tags the version and publishes the GitHub release with the entry as its body (section 8). Any other component skips the release |\n67: \n68: Global output lives under `.skilled/changelog/`. Versions read `v{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH}.{BUILD}`, the first one is `v1.0.0.0` and dates use `YYYY-MM-DD`. Between 40 and 79 percent confidence in the source or target, proceed and record the assumptions. Below 40 percent, stop and ask with A/B/C options.\n69: \n70: ---\n71: \n72: ## 4. HOW IT WORKS: CHANGELOG OUTPUT MODES\n73: \n74: ### Global Component Changelog\n75: \n76: A global entry is written to `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`.\n77: `{component}` is a lowercase kebab-case segment matching `^[a-z0-9]+(?:-[a-z0-9]+)*$`, or `{hub}/{link}` when the folder is a hub, with each segment matching that pattern.\n78: Reject an ambiguous hint rather than emit a guessed or underscore-bearing directory.\n79: The `v{VERSION}.md` filename is an exact version contract and is never slug-normalized.\n80: \n81: | Bump | Calculation | Source trigger intent |\n82: | --- | --- | --- |\n83: | `major` | `{MAJOR+1}.0.0.0` | Breaking, overhaul, rewrite, migration, platform version jump |\n84: | `minor` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR+1}.0.0` | Significant new feature or subsystem addition |\n85: | `patch` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH+1}.0` | Incremental improvement, bug fix, refactor, docs, cleanup |\n86: | `build` | `{MAJOR}.{MINOR}.{PATCH}.{BUILD+1}` | Hotfix, typo, same-day build on an already-published version |\n87: \n88: Auto detection takes the explicit `--bump` flag first, then change-type keywords in the `spec.md` title and purpose, then conventional commit prefixes. With no clear signal it defaults to `patch`. If the calculated file already exists, increment the build segment until the path is unique.\n89: \n90: ### Packet-Local Changelogs (Deprecated)\n91: \n92: Spec folders no longer get changelogs. Changelogs live only under `.skilled/changelog/` and in each skill's own `changelog/` folder. This workflow writes no file under `specs/**/changelog/` and refuses `--nested`.\n93: \n94: Existing packet-local files such as `changelog-<packet>-root.md` stay where they are and still validate. The spec-kit nested generator remains only to render them and prints a deprecation notice when it runs.\n95: \n96: ---\n97: \n98: ## 5. CHANGELOG FORMAT CONTRACT\n99: \n100: Read `assets/changelog-template.md` before generating global content. It owns the compact and expanded shapes (section 2), what to leave out and the say-it-once rule (section 3), voice and length (section 4) and format selection (section 5). Every entry must read in the voice and section shape of the three model entries, at whatever length the template's ceilings allow. `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.2.1.md` models sentence length, plain words, feature detail and the testing section. `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.1.0.md` models selection and a release with many changes, and `.skilled/changelog/skilled/v4.0.0.0.md` models voice and how a major release opens.\n101: \n102: The contract in one pass:\n103: \n104: 1. Frontmatter first, then an optional editorial title H1, then the summary narrative. No version header, backlink or version-date line.\n105: 2. Compact under 10 reader-visible changes when the release is neither major nor breaking: summary, `## What's New at a Glance` and `## Upgrade`, with `## Why This Release` only when the summary does not say why.\n106: 3. Expanded for 10 or more changes, a major bump or any breaking change: opening narrative, `## Why This Release`, `## What's New at a Glance`, topical H2 sections with benefit-led H4 items, `## How It Was Checked` and `## Upgrade Notes`.\n107: 4. A spec-folder blockquote, ``> Spec folder: `{path}` ``, when the release has a spec folder. It carries the path only, and the level stays in the packet.\n108: 5. Content chosen by reader impact and each fact said once. No file or schema tables unless the numbers are the story.\n109: 6. Detail by default, with no request needed. Each new feature says how it works and shows a real line of output. Every fix a reader could run into is named. `## How It Was Checked` gives the before and after, the cost and the tests that ran.\n110: 7. Short sentences in plain words: about 8 words each, none past 22 and 10 at most on average.\n111: \n112: When a source such as an older command snippet conflicts with the template, follow the template, write the narrative format and record the mismatch rather than inventing a hybrid.\n113: \n114: ### Frontmatter Contract\n115: \n116: A global entry must be as findable as a spec document, so its frontmatter block carries these five keys, in this order:\n117: \n118: | Key | Value |\n119: | --- | --- |\n120: | `title` | `<component> v<version>, <editorial title>`, or `<component> v<version>` when the entry has no editorial title. A Skilled release entry uses its H1, or `Skilled v<version>` |\n121: | `description` | One or two plain sentences, 250 characters at most, saying what the release changed |\n122: | `trigger_phrases` | The identity phrases first, then one or two topic phrases |\n123: | `importance_tier` | `\"normal\"` |\n124: | `contextType` | `\"general\"` |\n125: \n126: Identity phrases name the entry the way a reader asks for it.\n127: A skill entry declares `<component> v<version>` and `<component> <version>`, where `<component>` is the `name:` in the `SKILL.md` of the folder that holds the entry's changelog folder, never a `{hub}/{link}` target.\n128: The deep-loop runtime has no `SKILL.md` and is `deep-loop-runtime`.\n129: A Skilled release entry declares `v<version> release notes` and `skilled v<version>`.\n130: A topic phrase is 2 to 6 words from the entry's own text that name what changed.\n131: It carries no version number and never fits any release the way \"bug fixes\" would.\n132: A component entry may keep a `version:` key after the five.\n133: \n134: Existing packet-local entries keep the block the deprecated nested generator renders, with an identity phrase derived from the output path.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. TOPOLOGY AND TARGET RESOLUTION\n139: \n140: ### Mode Detection\n141: \n142: For a spec folder, read `implementation-summary.md` first and `spec.md` for the title, purpose and level. Read `tasks.md` only to confirm what shipped. Every source resolves to global mode:\n143: \n144: 1. `--nested` present: stop, say packet-local changelogs are deprecated and offer a global entry.\n145: 2. A spec folder, including a phase child, a phase parent or a folder that already has `changelog/`, resolves to the global component its changed paths touch (Global Component Discovery below).\n146: 3. A component hint or git history source is built from recent commits and the files they touched.\n147: \n148: ### Global Component Discovery\n149: \n150: Discover component folders from the repository at run time with `ls -d .skilled/changelog/*/ 2>/dev/null | sort`, never from a hardcoded table. Then resolve:\n151: \n152: 1. Each folder is a plain component name, such as `sk-doc` or `system-spec-kit`, with no numeric prefix. One folder is not a skill: `skilled` is the framework release line, with one entry per Skilled release.\n153: 2. Match changed paths against folder names by whole path segment, never by substring.\n154: 3. Match `.skilled/skills/{name}/**` to the folder named `{name}`, and `.skilled/commands/**` or `.skilled/agents/**` to the folder that owns them.\n155: 4. Resolve a hub to one of its links, never to the hub folder.\n156:    A hub is a real folder other than `skilled`, such as `sk-doc` or `sk-code`, holding one link per member packet plus `parent`, the hub's own changelog.\n157:    `ls -l .skilled/changelog/{hub}/` lists them.\n158:    A path under `.skilled/skills/{hub}/{packet}/` resolves to the link whose target is `{packet}/changelog`.\n159:    Any other path under the hub, and a hint that names the hub, resolve to `{hub}/parent`.\n160:    A hint that names a member by its link, its packet folder or `{hub}/{link}` resolves to that link.\n161: 5. Match `component_hint` against folder names exactly.\n162: 6. Use spec path segments as a tiebreaker.\n163: 7. Resolve to `skilled` only when `component_hint` names it. A changed path never selects it, because the operator chooses a framework release.\n164: 8. If nothing matches, do not invent or default a folder. Pause and ask which component the changelog belongs to.\n165: \n166: Then choose the target:\n167: \n168: 1. A component with more than 60 percent of the changed files is primary.\n169: 2. With several roughly equal components, pick the primary by file count and list the rest as secondary.\n170: 3. Verify the target folder exists before writing, and never create a missing one.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 7. CREATION WORKFLOW\n175: \n176: Complete these seven steps in order. The command YAMLs number the same workflow as eight steps, because they run the optional GitHub release as its own step 7.\n177: \n178: Before writing, validate each authored component, packet or phase slug. Exact version filenames and frozen changelog paths stay exempt.\n179: \n180: ```bash\n181: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/check_authored_name_kebab.py <component-or-phase-slug>\n182: ```\n183: \n184: 1. **Analyze context.** Resolve the source type and read the source (section 6). Compile `work_context`: what changed for the reader, the change type, the level and the source.\n185: 2. **Resolve output target.** Discover the folders, match paths and hints (section 6), choose the primary component, list secondary ones and verify `.skilled/changelog/{resolved_folder}/` exists.\n186: 3. **Determine version.** Do these in order:\n187:    - List the `v*.md` entries in the target folder and in its generation folders (`v1+/`, `v2+/` and so on).\n188:    - List the version entries on the upstream branch with `git ls-tree`, which reads the branch's upstream, else `origin/main`.\n189:    For the `skilled` release line with at least one entry, first check the highest entry for a release tag with `git ls-remote --exit-code --tags origin refs/tags/v{latest}`:\n190:    - Exit 2 means no tag, so that release is still open. Extend that entry wherever it sits instead of numbering a new one, keep its version and skip the bump.\n191:    - A bump the operator chose is not applied, and the report says so, and says that tagging the open release first starts a new one.\n192:    - When the open entry exists only upstream, stop and ask for a merge or rebase first.\n193:    - Any exit other than 0 or 2 means the check failed, so number a new entry and say so rather than risk editing a release that already shipped.\n194:    Every other component numbers a new entry.\n195:    - Start from the highest of the local and upstream `vX.Y.Z.B` versions, choose the bump (section 4) and calculate the next version.\n196:    - Increment the build segment while the file exists locally or upstream, because two branches numbering from their own checkout pick the same version and collide at merge.\n197:    - Without a reachable upstream, number locally and say so in the report.\n198: 4. **Generate content.** Read `assets/changelog-template.md`, then:\n199:    - Write the frontmatter block per the Frontmatter Contract in section 5.\n200:    - Choose the content before writing it (template section 3). Start from what the reader would notice, act on or decide differently about, confirm each against the source, merge items with the same effect and drop the housekeeping.\n201:    - Select compact or expanded from the changes that survive (template section 5).\n202:    - Write each section to its one job, so a fact appears once: themes in the opening, the gap in Why This Release, one payoff line per theme at a glance, the detail in the H4 items and only actions in the upgrade notes.\n203:    - Explain how each new feature works, step by step, and quote one real line of its output. Name every fix a reader could run into.\n204:    - Write `## How It Was Checked` from evidence the source records: the before and after of a real command, the measured cost and each suite's result. Run the command when the source lacks its output. Never write a number that no run produced, and say so when something was not measured.\n205:    - Write short sentences in everyday words (template section 4). Split a long sentence rather than cut its fact.\n206:    - Lead with why the release matters. Outside How It Was Checked, add a table only when its numbers are the story.\n207:    - Order the opening, the glance list, the topical sections and the upgrade notes by what matters most to the reader, never by when the work landed.\n208:    - When extending an open release, read the entry first and fold the new work into it.\n209:      Add its section or H4 items, a glance bullet in section order, its spec folder on the spec-folder line and any upgrade action.\n210:      Widen the opening and Why This Release only enough to cover the new theme, keep the title unless the new work changes the headline, and correct any existing sentence the new work makes untrue.\n211:      When the new work matters more to a reader than what the entry leads with, retitle the entry and re-rank its sections, so the release still opens on its most important change.\n212: 5. **Validate quality.** Run the section 9 checks on the draft. For a new entry, confirm the version is strictly greater than the latest one and no file exists at the target. For an extension, confirm the target is the open entry. Confirm every path the entry names exists. Apply the cut test (template section 3), fix what is safe and revalidate.\n213: 6. **Write the file.** Write `.skilled/changelog/{primary_component}/v{next_version}.md`, or edit the open entry in place when extending, then read back its first lines.\n214:    Set `next_version` in every version carrier the component's skill root already has (`SKILL.md`, `description.json`, `hub-router.json` and `mode-registry.json`), never in `README.md` or a hub `ROUTER.md`, whose versions are each document's own.\n215:    For a hub, run `node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/parent-skill-check.cjs` on the hub and stop on a non-zero exit.\n216:    Report secondary components as candidates rather than writing extra files.\n217: 7. **Report and preserve context.** Report the path, component, version, whether the entry is new or extended, bump type, summary, section count and H4 item count. For a `skilled` entry, also report the release: its URL when one is published, or that none is and the `--release` run that publishes it. When the entry belongs to a published release, bring the release in step first (section 8). A spec-folder source can preserve context through the normal memory save workflow. Do not claim completion before the written file is verified.\n218: \n219: Pause when confidence drops below 40 percent, component resolution stays ambiguous, the calculated version looks wrong or a required section would be empty.\n220: \n221: ---\n222: \n223: ## 8. NOTATION AND FORMAT RULES\n224: \n225: The voice, structure, omission and length rules live in `assets/changelog-template.md` sections 3 and 4. The non-negotiables are:\n226: \n227: 1. Write for a smart person who is not a developer, and lead with why the release matters.\n228: 2. Keep what the reader would notice or act on, how each feature works, every fix and the evidence in How It Was Checked. Drop file inventories, test counts outside that section, internal labels, process detail and follow-on housekeeping, and compress reverted work to one story sentence or nothing.\n229: 3. Say each fact once, in the section whose job it is.\n230: 4. Bold lead-in sentences on glance bullets, a trailing `<!-- section: {Topical Domain} -->` comment on each one in an expanded entry, H4 item headings of 2-7 words (10 at most), an `&nbsp;` line before every H2 (the opening, Why This Release, What's New at a Glance and every later section), nothing between H4 items, no `---` rule in the body and inline `**Breaking:**` markers.\n231: 5. No Oxford commas, em dashes or semicolons, per the Human Voice Rules.\n232: 6. Short sentences in everyday words. No sentence runs past 22 words and the entry averages 10 at most.\n233: \n234: ### Release Notes\n235: \n236: For GitHub release notes, use the changelog content with any YAML frontmatter and the editorial title H1 removed, keeping its spacing, then append an `&nbsp;` line and the pointer. `scripts/build_release_notes.py <entry> --out <file>` builds exactly this body, so a publish and every later amend match the entry:\n237: \n238: ```text\n239: &nbsp;\n240: \n241: Full changelog: `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md`\n242: ```\n243: \n244: The command YAMLs own the release itself, as `step_7_publish_release`.\n245: They run it only when `publish_release` is explicitly true and the component is `skilled`, the framework release line.\n246: Any other component skips the release, because component versions share the `vX.X.X.X` shape with release tags and a component version must never become one.\n247: The tag is `v{next_version}`, and the release title is the tag followed by the entry's editorial title, the H1 without its leading version.\n248: The step checks for a tag collision and an authenticated `gh`, then runs `git tag -a`, `git push origin {release_tag}` and `gh release create {release_tag} --title {release_title} --notes-file {notes_file}`, which publishes at once with no draft stage.\n249: `:confirm` shows the exact commands and runs them only after approval.\n250: \n251: #### Keep a Published Release in Step\n252: \n253: A published release must always show its entry as it reads now. Whenever an entry changes after its release is published, bring the release in step in the same run, without waiting to be asked:\n254: \n255: 1. Rebuild the body with `build_release_notes.py`.\n256: 2. Run `gh release edit {release_tag} --notes-file {notes_file}`, adding `--title {release_title}` when the editorial title changed.\n257: 3. Check the tag: `git show {release_tag}:{entry_path}` must match the entry on the default branch.\n258: 4. Report the release URL from `gh release view {release_tag} --json url`.\n259: \n260: When step 3 shows a difference, the tag points at a commit that holds older text or older code. Recommend a republish, so the tag, the entry and the release agree.\n261: \n262: Republishing deletes and recreates a public ref, so it needs the operator's yes each time. Never run it as a default or in an unattended run. Ask once, in the same reply as the report, with republish as the recommended answer. Name the old tag object and say it can be pushed back. Only a request that names the tag move counts as the yes, such as \"republish the tag\" or \"move the tag\". A request to publish or edit the notes does not. The body edit in step 2 needs no such wait.\n263: \n264: After the yes, run these commands in order. The local tag goes too, or `git tag -a` fails because it still exists:\n265: \n266: ```bash\n267: gh release delete {release_tag} --cleanup-tag --yes\n268: git tag -d {release_tag}\n269: git tag -a {release_tag} -m \"{tag_message}\"\n270: git push origin {release_tag}\n271: gh release create {release_tag} --title \"{release_title}\" --notes-file {notes_file} --latest --verify-tag\n272: ```\n273: \n274: ---\n275: \n276: ## 9. VALIDATION\n277: \n278: Validate the target, version and content before writing.\n279: \n280: Global changelog checks:\n281: \n282: 1. The target is `.skilled/changelog/{component}/v{VERSION}.md` in an existing folder, and no file exists at that path. Extending an open `skilled` release is the one exception: the target is that release's existing entry.\n283: 2. The version follows `vX.Y.Z.B` and is strictly greater than the latest one in the folder, counting its generation folders. An extension keeps the open release's version.\n284: 3. The frontmatter carries `title`, `description`, `trigger_phrases`, `importance_tier` and `contextType` in contract order, with both identity phrases for the target path and at least one topic phrase. `validate_document.py` blocks an entry whose block, keys or phrases are missing.\n285: 4. The prose opens with the summary narrative, and the spec-folder blockquote is present when the source is a spec folder.\n286: 5. The tier's sections are present: compact carries the summary, `## What's New at a Glance` and `## Upgrade`. Expanded carries the opening narrative, `## Why This Release`, `## What's New at a Glance`, at least one topical H2 with H4 items, `## How It Was Checked` and `## Upgrade Notes`.\n287: 6. No Files Changed, Test Impact or Schema Changes section. Any table outside How It Was Checked passes the earned-evidence rule.\n288: 7. Omission applied: no file inventories, process detail or housekeeping items, and no test counts outside How It Was Checked. No internal labels (task, requirement, scenario or finding IDs, packet or phase numbers) appear outside the spec-folder line.\n289: 8. Each fact appears once: no sentence restates one from another section, and each glance bullet adds something the summary did not.\n290: 9. The template's ceilings hold: opening within 5 paragraphs, prose near its word budgets (guidance, counted in words, never sentences), at most 14 glance bullets, H4 headings within 10 words, compact files within 40 prose lines, expanded files within 280 lines below the frontmatter and expanded entries within 32 H4 items.\n291: 10. H4 headings are benefit-led and never numbered or sentence-length, and no H3 sits inside a topical section.\n292: 11. `scripts/check_changelog_shape.py` passes the draft. It fails an expanded entry past 280 lines or 32 H4 items, more than 14 glance bullets, tagged glance bullets out of section order and a spec-folder line with a level label. It also fails a sentence past 22 words, an average past 10 words a sentence and an expanded entry with no `## How It Was Checked`.\n293: \n294: Voice gate, run on the draft before writing:\n295: \n296: ```bash\n297: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-with-human-voice/scripts/hvr_scan.py <draft-file>\n298: ```\n299: \n300: The scan must report zero hard blockers (banned punctuation or words).\n301: Deductions below that threshold are acceptable.\n302: If the scanner is unavailable, apply the rules by hand and record that the scan was skipped.\n303: Structural checks complement it:\n304: - bold lead-ins on glance bullets.\n305: - an `&nbsp;` line before every H2.\n306: - nothing between H4 items.\n307: - no `---` rule in the body.\n308: - no `**Problem:**`/`**Fix:**` labels.\n309: - inline `**Breaking:**` markers.\n310: \n311: Shape gate, run on the draft before writing:\n312: \n313: ```bash\n314: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/scripts/check_changelog_shape.py <draft-file>\n315: ```\n316: \n317: Exit 1 prints one line per broken rule and blocks the write. Cut at the H4 level (template section 3) and run it again. The order rule is skipped, with a note, when no glance bullet carries a section tag.\n318: \n319: After writing, run the shared markdown checks:\n320: \n321: ```bash\n322: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/validate_document.py <written-file>\n323: python3 .skilled/skills/sk-doc/shared/scripts/extract_structure.py <written-file>\n324: ```\n325: \n326: If validation fails, fix the blocking issues or report the exact blocker and command output.\n327: \n328: Packet-local files are deprecated, so no new one is validated here. `validate_document.py` still checks the existing ones under `specs/**/changelog/`.\n329: \n330: ---\n331: \n332: ## 10. RULES\n333: \n334: ### ✅ ALWAYS\n335: \n336: 1. Follow the seven-step workflow in order.\n337: 2. Read `assets/changelog-template.md` before generating global content.\n338: 3. Discover global component folders from `.skilled/changelog/` at run time, and resolve to an existing one before writing.\n339: 4. Keep global versions four-part and strictly sequential. Extending an untagged `skilled` release keeps its version.\n340: 5. Choose content by reader impact and say each fact once.\n341: 6. Refuse `--nested` and route a spec-folder source to a global component entry.\n342: 7. List secondary affected components.\n343: 8. Verify the written file before claiming completion.\n344: 9. Keep this packet self-contained, with the advisor identity at the `sk-doc` hub root.\n345: 10. Write the feature detail, the fixes and How It Was Checked without being asked for them.\n346: 11. Bring a published release in step with its entry in the same run, and end the report with the release URL.\n347: \n348: ### ⛔ NEVER\n349: \n350: 1. Add a packet-local `graph-metadata.json`.\n351: 2. Overwrite an existing changelog or reuse an existing version. Extending an untagged `skilled` release entry is the one exception, and it adds to the entry rather than replacing it.\n352: 3. Guess a component folder, or create a missing one.\n353: 4. Write an entry with an empty summary or an empty required section.\n354: 5. Write a new packet-local changelog, or use the deprecated nested generator to create one. Its `--write` only re-renders an existing packet changelog.\n355: 6. Make the command router or YAML the only workflow contract.\n356: 7. Hide a source-format conflict. Record it and follow the template.\n357: \n358: ### ⚠️ ESCALATE IF\n359: \n360: 1. No source context can be found in a spec folder, component history or git history.\n361: 2. Component resolution stays ambiguous after file-count and path-segment analysis.\n362: 3. The calculated version is unexpected or cannot be made unique.\n363: 4. A required section would be empty.\n364: 5. The user asks for a GitHub release with no git release workflow or command context available.\n365: 6. Validation still fails after safe local fixes.\n366: \n367: ---\n368: \n369: ## 11. REFERENCES\n370: \n371: 1. `assets/changelog-template.md`: the canonical format, selection, voice and length rules.\n372: 2. `scripts/check_changelog_shape.py`: the shape gate that enforces the expanded ceilings, the glance order and the spec-folder line.\n373:    `scripts/build_release_notes.py`: builds the GitHub release body from an entry, for a publish and for keeping a published release in step.\n374: 3. `references/README.md`: the route map to `worked-examples.md` (filled-in entries), `version-bump-rules.md` (concrete version choices) and `topology-edge-cases.md` (placement, back-dating, source conflicts and the release flow).\n375: 4. `.skilled/commands/create/changelog.md`: the thin `/create:changelog` router.\n376: 5. `.skilled/commands/create/assets/create-changelog-auto.yaml` and `create-changelog-confirm.yaml`: the `:auto` and `:confirm` workflows, which resolve to this same contract.\n377: \n378: ---\n379: \n380: ## 12. SUCCESS CRITERIA\n381: \n382: The workflow succeeds when:\n383: \n384: 1. The file lands at its global component path, and a `--nested` request is refused with a global entry offered instead.\n385: 2. Global versioning is sequential, unique and four-part, and an untagged `skilled` release is extended rather than followed by a second untagged entry.\n386: 3. The entry follows the template's compact or expanded shape and reads in the v4 style: why first, benefit-led headings, short plain sentences, how each feature works, every fix, the evidence in How It Was Checked and each fact said once.\n387: 4. A release body uses the entry and appends the full changelog path, and a published release never shows older text than its entry.\n388: 5. Validation passes, or the remaining issue is escalated with exact evidence.\n389: 6. No other file changes unless the user asked for it."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-changelog/SKILL.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794585754425,"end":1794585755577}}}}
